---
title: "Hybrid retrieval"
description: "Combine dense semantic and sparse lexical candidates in QQL, then fuse them with RRF or DBSF for higher-recall ranked results."
sidebar:
  order: 1
---

Hybrid retrieval combines semantic dense similarity with lexical sparse
matching. QQL exposes a shorthand and an explicit multi-stage form.

{% aside type="tip" title="On Qdrant 1.20+? Prefer server BM25" %}
When the text is already in the payload, a remote Qdrant 1.20+ server can
score BM25 itself with `QUERY BM25 '…' USING <field>` — no sparse vector, no
embedding step. The sparse examples on this page are **client sparse BM25**,
which stays the path for edge and Qdrant < 1.20. See
[Server BM25 on Qdrant 1.20](/docs/guides/bm25-server/) and
[Migrate sparse BM25 to server BM25](/docs/guides/migrate-sparse-bm25/).
{% /aside %}

## Shorthand

{% qqlExample title="Schema-resolved hybrid search" %}
```sql
QUERY HYBRID TEXT 'zero downtime deployment' DENSE dense SPARSE sparse FUSION RRF
FROM runbooks
WHERE service = 'gateway'
LIMIT 10;
```
{% /qqlExample %}

If the collection has exactly one dense and one sparse vector, omit the names:

{% qqlExample title="Hybrid defaults" %}
```sql
QUERY HYBRID TEXT 'zero downtime deployment' FUSION RRF FROM runbooks LIMIT 10;
```
{% /qqlExample %}

## Explicit candidate stages

{% qqlExample title="Weighted RRF pipeline" %}
```sql
WITH
  semantic AS (QUERY 'zero downtime deployment' USING dense WHERE service = 'gateway' LIMIT 120),
  lexical AS (QUERY 'zero downtime deployment' USING sparse WHERE service = 'gateway' LIMIT 180)
QUERY FUSION RRF
FROM runbooks
PREFETCH (semantic, lexical)
PARAMS (rrf_k = 40, rrf_weights = [0.7, 0.3])
LIMIT 10;
```
{% /qqlExample %}

Use the explicit form when candidate limits, filters, or weights differ by
stage. `rrf_weights` length must equal the prefetch count.

## Client sparse BM25

`USING bm25` without an explicit model resolves to the `Qdrant/bm25` sparse
model — queries embed with unit weights and documents with tf saturation,
staying wire-compatible with Qdrant's `qdrant/bm25`. This leg searches a
sparse vector, so it runs on edge and on any Qdrant version:

{% qqlExample title="Client sparse bm25 defaults" %}
```sql
QUERY 'zero downtime deployment' FROM runbooks USING bm25 LIMIT 10;
```
{% /qqlExample %}

For a server-scored lexical leg on Qdrant 1.20+, swap it for
`QUERY BM25 '…' USING <field>` inside `PREFETCH`; see
[Hybrid: dense + server BM25](/docs/guides/bm25-server/#hybrid-dense--server-bm25).

## RRF or DBSF?

| Fusion | Prefer when |
|---|---|
| RRF | Ranking positions are reliable but score scales differ |
| DBSF | Normalized score magnitude carries useful information |
